  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Early This Morning: Lingering snow and slick travel conditions\n   continue mainly south of the Highway 12 corridor and across\n   higher mountain passes, with Winter Weather Advisories expiring\n   at 5 AM and 8 AM MST.\n\n-  Today & Tonight: Areas of valley fog this morning, with patchy\n   fog redeveloping late tonight into Sunday morning.\n\n-  Sunday Night - Monday: Another round of wintry precipitation \n   brings light valley snow to northwest Montana, a rain/snow mix \n   to west-central valleys, and renewed pass impacts.\n\n-  Tuesday - Wednesday: A brief break with drier conditions under\n   building high pressure before another weak disturbance \n   approaches around Thursday.\n\nThe overnight weather system is beginning to wind down early this morning,\nwith the steadiest precipitation gradually shifting east. Remaining snow\nshowers continue to impact higher elevations and travel corridors mainly\nalong and south of Highway 12, including Lolo Pass, Lost Trail Pass, and\nthe Butte/Blackfoot region. Winter Weather Advisories remain in effect\nuntil 5 AM MST for the Bitterroot/Sapphire Mountains, Southern Clearwater\nMountains, and the Butte/Blackfoot region, and until 8 AM MST for eastern\nLemhi County and surrounding passes.\n\nBehind the exiting system, cool northwesterly flow will keep scattered\nlight showers lingering over the mountains through the day, while valleys\nsee mostly cloudy skies and gradual afternoon clearing across southwest\nMontana and Lemhi County. With abundant low-level moisture left behind,\nareas of valley fog will persist through late morning before lifting. As\nskies partially clear tonight and overnight lows dip into the upper 20s\nacross western Montana valleys, patchy fog is expected to redevelop late\ntonight into Sunday morning.\n\nClouds thicken through the day Sunday ahead of the next wave of Pacific\nmoisture arriving in the afternoon and persisting into Monday night. Across\nnorthwest Montana, snow levels will start near valley floors and remain low\nthrough Monday morning, allowing for light valley snow that could bring one\nhalf to 1 inch of accumulation for the morning commute. Farther south into\nwest-central Montana valleys, a rain/snow mix Sunday night into Monday\nmorning should transition to mainly rain by Monday afternoon as\ntemperatures warm into the upper 30s to near 40 degrees. While overall\nliquid amounts remain light, mountain passes will once again see wintry\ndriving conditions Sunday night through Tuesday.\n\nLooking into midweek, a ridge of high pressure builds over the Northern\nRockies Tuesday into Wednesday, bringing a couple of drier days with\nseasonable temperatures and renewed potential for valley fog and low\nstratus. Ensemble guidance suggests another quick-hitting disturbance could\nclip the region around Thursday with light precipitation.",
